course: dictionary
lesson: 12914
title: Esteem: respect and good opinion
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Esteem means great respect and a good opinion of someone. When people hold you in high esteem, they think well of you and value what you do. «The team held their captain in high esteem, because she always helped new players.» The clue is always helped: the team respects her a lot.
explain: Self-esteem is how much you value and respect yourself. «Ravi felt proud after finishing his first book, and his self-esteem grew.» Ravi felt good about himself, so self-esteem means feeling good about who you are.
explain: Esteem can also be a verb. To esteem someone means to respect and admire them a lot. «Mrs Patel esteems her students for their kindness.» The clue is kindness, which is what she admires. Try swapping in respects and admires: the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Esteem means great respect and good opinion of someone.
fact: Self-esteem means how much you value and respect yourself.
fact: «Held in high esteem» means people respect someone a lot.
fact: To esteem someone (formal) means to respect and admire them a lot.
fact: Esteem is uncountable as a noun, so you can say «a token of our esteem» to mean a gift that shows respect.
exercise: «The villagers held the old doctor in high esteem.» What does held in high esteem mean? | *They respected her a lot | They gave her a new house | They forgot her name | They were afraid of her
exercise: «Ruby's self-esteem went up when she won the poem contest.» What is self-esteem? | A type of sports prize | *How much you value and respect yourself | How many friends you have | Speed at running
exercise: «The class esteemed their teacher for her patience.» Find and copy the word that means respected and admired. | *esteemed | teacher | class | patience
test: «Our school sent a card as a token of their esteem.» What does esteem mean here? | Cheap paper | *Respect and good opinion | Being late | A type of school lunch
test: Which phrase means that people respect someone a lot? | Low self-esteem | *Held in high esteem | A token of sorrow | Strange to look at
exam: Which sentence uses esteem as a verb? | «Her esteem was very large.» | «The esteem was made of wood.» | «The room was full of esteem.» | *«We esteem our grandparents for their wisdom.»
